  "RE: Accommodation Request of Dana Whitcombe; Harbor Point Logistics, Inc.": ["other", "framing"],
  "Question Presented": ["heading", "framing"],
  "Under the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, as amended, 42 U.S.C. §§ 12101–12213, and the parallel California and New York statutes, must Harbor Point Logistics, Inc. permit Dana Whitcombe, a senior dispatcher with Crohn’s disease, to work remotely four days per week?": [
    "issue",
    "law"
  ],
  "Short Answer": ["heading", "framing"],
  "Probably yes.": ["conclusion", "conclusion"],
  "Whitcombe’s Crohn’s disease is almost certainly a disability, because the ADA Amendments Act of 2008 directs that the definition be construed broadly and in favor of coverage. Pub. L. No. 110-325, § 2(b)(5), 122 Stat. 3553, 3554 (2008).": [
    "conclusion",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "Whether four remote days is a reasonable accommodation is a closer question, but Harbor Point’s own telework history makes it likely that a court would find the request reasonable.": [
    "conclusion",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "Relevant Facts": ["heading", "framing"],
  "Whitcombe has worked for Harbor Point, a New York corporation, as a senior dispatcher in its Oakland, California office since 2019.": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "She was diagnosed with Crohn’s disease in 2017; during flares she experiences urgent bowel symptoms several times an hour and fatigue that lasts for days. Whitcombe Decl. ¶¶ 3–5.": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "Her symptoms are controlled most weeks with biologic medication, but she had six flares in the past year.": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "From March 2020 to June 2022, all eleven dispatchers, including Whitcombe, worked remotely, and the company’s on-time delivery rate rose by two percent. Ex. B at 2.": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "In August 2026, Whitcombe asked to work from home four days per week; her manager denied the request in a two-line email stating that dispatchers “must be on the floor to coordinate with drivers in real time.” Ex. C.": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "Discussion": ["heading", "framing"],
  "The ADA prohibits a covered employer from failing to make “reasonable accommodations to the known physical or mental limitations of an otherwise qualified individual with a disability,” unless the accommodation would impose an undue hardship. 42 U.S.C. § 12112(b)(5)(A).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "Whitcombe’s claim therefore turns on two questions: whether she has a disability, and whether four remote days is a reasonable accommodation that Harbor Point cannot show would be an undue hardship.": [
    "issue",
    "law",
    "roadmap"
  ],
  "California and New York law impose parallel duties, Cal. Gov't Code § 12940(m); N.Y. Exec. Law § 296(3)(a), and this memo addresses them after the federal claim.": [
    "roadmap",
    "law",
    "rule"
  ],
  "Because Harbor Point is a private employer, the Equal Protection Clause, U.S. Const. amend. XIV, § 1, plays no role.": [
    "application",
    "application"
  ],
  "Harbor Point will likely be required to grant the request.": ["conclusion", "conclusion"],
  "A. Whitcombe’s Crohn’s disease is a disability under the amended ADA.": [
    "heading",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "The ADA defines a disability as “a physical or mental impairment that substantially limits one or more major life activities of such individual.” 42 U.S.C. § 12102(1)(A).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "Major life activities include “the operation of a major bodily function,” including “digestive” and “bowel” functions. Id. § 12102(2)(B).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "An impairment that is episodic or in remission is a disability “if it would substantially limit a major life activity when active.” Id. § 12102(4)(D).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "The definition of disability in this chapter shall be construed in favor of broad coverage of individuals under this chapter, to the maximum extent permitted by the terms of this chapter. 42 U.S.C. § 12102(4)(A).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "Congress added these rules of construction to reject the demanding standard of Toyota Motor Mfg., Ky., Inc. v. Williams, 534 U.S. 184, 198 (2002), which had required that an impairment limit activities “of central importance to most people’s daily lives.” See Pub. L. No. 110-325, § 2(b)(4), 122 Stat. at 3554; H.R. Rep. No. 110-730, pt. 1, at 5 (2008).": [
    "explanation",
    "precedent"
  ],
  "The EEOC’s regulations implement that mandate: “substantially limits” is “not meant to be a demanding standard,” 29 C.F.R. § 1630.2(j)(1)(i), and an impairment need not prevent or severely restrict a major life activity to qualify, id. § 1630.2(j)(1)(ii).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "The regulations list impairments that will “in virtually all cases” substantially limit a major life activity, and the list is illustrative rather than exhaustive. See 29 C.F.R. § 1630.2(j)(3)(iii); 76 Fed. Reg. 16,978, 16,981 (Mar. 25, 2011).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "In Summers v. Altarum Institute, Corp., 740 F.3d 325, 329 (4th Cir. 2014), the Fourth Circuit held that even a temporary impairment may qualify if it is sufficiently severe, because Congress meant the amendments to make coverage easier to establish.": [
    "explanation",
    "precedent"
  ],
  "Here, Whitcombe’s flares substantially limit the operation of her digestive and bowel functions, and her symptoms are episodic in the way § 12102(4)(D) contemplates.": [
    "application",
    "application"
  ],
  "Like the plaintiff in Summers, she need not show a permanent limitation, and the regulations direct that mitigating measures such as her biologic medication be disregarded. 29 C.F.R. § 1630.2(j)(1)(vi).": [
    "application",
    "application"
  ],
  "Harbor Point is therefore very likely to concede, or a court to find, that she has a disability.": [
    "conclusion",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "B. Remote work four days per week is likely a reasonable accommodation.": [
    "heading",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "A reasonable accommodation may include “job restructuring, part-time or modified work schedules,” and similar measures. 42 U.S.C. § 12111(9)(B).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "The employer must engage in an “informal, interactive process” with the employee to identify an effective accommodation. 29 C.F.R. § 1630.2(o)(3); see also 29 C.F.R. pt. 1630, app. § 1630.9.": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "The EEOC’s guidance treats telework as a form of reasonable accommodation where the essential functions can be performed at home. EEOC, Enforcement Guidance: Reasonable Accommodation and Undue Hardship Under the Americans with Disabilities Act, Question 34 (Oct. 17, 2002).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "Courts have disagreed, however, about how much weight an employer’s judgment about in-person attendance deserves.": [
    "explanation",
    "law"
  ],
  "In EEOC v. Ford Motor Co., 782 F.3d 753, 762–63 (6th Cir. 2015) (en banc), the Sixth Circuit held that regular in-person attendance was an essential function of a resale buyer’s job, deferring to the employer’s judgment that the role required face-to-face collaboration. Id. at 762.": [
    "explanation",
    "precedent"
  ],
  "The Supreme Court has explained that an accommodation is reasonable if it “seems reasonable on its face, i.e., ordinarily or in the run of cases.” US Airways, Inc. v. Barnett, 535 U.S. 391, 401–02 (2002).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "Commentators have argued that post-pandemic experience undercuts employer claims that physical presence is essential. Jane Roe, Rethinking Essential Functions, 100 Harv. L. Rev. 1, 15 (1987); see also 1 Helen R. Castellano et al., Workplace Accommodation Law § 13.03 (5th ed. 2012).": [
    "explanation",
    "law"
  ],
  "Here, Harbor Point’s denial rests on the same kind of judgment the employer asserted in Ford Motor, but the record is different.": [
